            <div xml:id="d329">

                <pb facs="0443" n="371" sameAs="frus:frus1909/pg371"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 21274/25–31.</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Brown to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Tegucigalpa</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1909-10-14"><hi rendition="#italic">October 14,
                        1909</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 151.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my No. 144, of the 6th
                    instant, I have the honor to transmit herewith copies of further correspondence
                    on the subject of the interpretation and application of the Honduranean laws of
                    navigation in the specific instance of the schooner <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >George A. Lawry</hi>, of New York, owned by Consular Agent Glynn, at
                    Trujillo.</p>
                <p>As set forth in the notes from the ministry for foreign affairs, dated the 9th
                    and 14th instant, respectively, replying to the legation’s notes on this
                    subject, the question at issue would now seem to be satisfactorily and
                    definitely settled, and that there will be no further difficulties encountered
                    by American vessels owned by American citizens temporarily residing in
                    Honduras.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Philip Brown.</hi></signed></closer>




